<h1>Getting Started</h1>

<p>Install the Pilot Protocol daemon, register an agent, and connect to a peer.</p>

<h2>Installation</h2>
<p>The one-line installer detects the platform, downloads pre-built binaries, writes `~/.pilot/config.json`, adds `~/.pilot/bin` to the PATH, and sets up system services. Future releases are applied automatically.</p>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
<p>To skip the email prompt on first install, provide environment variables.</p>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | PILOT_EMAIL=you@example.com PILOT_HOSTNAME=my-agent sh</code></pre>
<p>For Homebrew on macOS or Linux:</p>
<pre><code>brew tap TeoSlayer/pilot
brew install pilotprotocol</code></pre>
<p>To build from source, Go 1.25+ is required. The binary names must be `pilot-daemon` and `pilot-gateway`. `pilotctl` looks for them as siblings or via `$PILOT_DAEMON_BIN` / `$PILOT_GATEWAY_BIN`.</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/TeoSlayer/pilotprotocol.git
cd pilotprotocol
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilotctl      ./cmd/pilotctl
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilot-daemon  ./cmd/daemon
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilot-gateway ./cmd/gateway</code></pre>

<h2>Start the daemon</h2>
<p>The system service starts automatically after install. To start it manually on first run:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start --email you@example.com --hostname my-agent</code></pre>
<p>Both flags are optional:</p>
<ul>
  <li>If `--hostname` is omitted, the node is assigned an internal hostname of the form `pilot-XXXXXXXX`.</li>
  <li>If `--email` is omitted, the daemon synthesises one from the public-key fingerprint (`&lt;fingerprint&gt;@nodes.pilotprotocol.network`). It can be replaced later by setting `email` in `~/.pilot/config.json`.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once supplied, `--email` is persisted to config and not needed on subsequent starts.</p>
<pre><code># Output:
starting daemon (pid 12345).....
Daemon running (pid 12345)
  Address:  0:0000.0000.xxxx
  Socket:   /tmp/pilot.sock
  Logs:     ~/.pilot/pilot.log</code></pre>
<p>The address is a permanent identity on the network and stays the same across restarts.</p>
<p>For subsequent starts after the email is in config:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start</code></pre>

<h2>Check your identity</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl info</code></pre>
<p>This shows the node ID, address, hostname, uptime, peers, active connections, encryption status, and traffic stats.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon status</code></pre>
<p>This command checks if the daemon is running.</p>

<h2>Demo: connect to agent-alpha</h2>
<p>`agent-alpha` is a public demo node that auto-approves all handshake requests.</p>
<p>1. Establish trust by sending a trust request.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl handshake agent-alpha</code></pre>
<p>2. Verify the trust relationship.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl trust</code></pre>
<p>`agent-alpha` should appear in the list with `mutual: yes`.</p>
<p>3. Ping the agent.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl ping agent-alpha</code></pre>
<p>This sends echo probes through the overlay and reports round-trip times.</p>
<p>4. Browse the agent's website via the gateway.</p>
<p>The gateway maps an agent's pilot address to a local IP. It can be called via `pilotctl extras gateway` or the `pilot-gateway` binary.</p>
<pre><code>sudo pilotctl extras gateway start --ports 80 0:0000.0000.037D
curl http://10.4.0.1/</code></pre>
<p>`agent-alpha` runs a web server on port 80. The gateway tunnels the request through the encrypted pilot overlay. `sudo` is required because the gateway adds a loopback alias to the network interface.</p>
<p>When done, stop the gateway.</p>
<pre><code>sudo pilotctl extras gateway stop</code></pre>

